<h2>Dedicated Astrophotography Set Up</h2>

<p>CUAS has an Askar 91F Refractor, along with a 2600MC ASIAIR Colour Camera and HEQ5 Equatorial Mount for Astrophotography, available for member use. 
To get access to this Astrophotography setup, you must first pass the Northumberland ObsTest, and then sign up to the Astrophotography Training List from the Observing Portal.
You must read the Astrophotography Guide in its entirety in advance. You will then have the opportunity to attend a one-on-one nighttime training session, where one of us will supervise
you setting up the equipment and answer any questions. If we deem you ready and sufficiently careful, you will be allowed access to the equipment.
</p>

<p>Be sure that you are interested in astrophotography enough to pursue this hobby. It
will take quite some independent effort to learn it from scratch, and it uses a lot of our
time to train people who do not put their own time in and don’t end up using the
equipment.</p>


<h3>Askar 91F Flat-Field Refractor</h3>
<p> This is the 91mm aperture, 609mm focal length F6.7
telescope, which acts to gather lots of starlight and focus it onto the camera sensor. 
It includes the focussing mechanism, as well as attachment points to the mount and camera.
The manual is available at <a href="https://www.sharpstar-optics.com/download_Detail_1/63.html">Askar 91F Manual</a>.</p>

<h3> ZWO ASI2600MC Air</h3>
<p> This is our all-in-one smart one-shot-colour (OSC) camera. It has two
sensors, including an 26 megapixel APS-C imaging sensor and a smaller guide sensor. It is
a cooled camera, which can reduce the sensor temperature to 30-35°C below ambient,
which significantly reduces dark current noise.
It contains a mini computer and is entirely controlled wirelessly via a dedicated Wi-Fi network
and the ASIAIR app. It interfaces directly with the mount, and has feed-through power to the
mount and dew heater, so the entire setup can run off one plug.
The manual is available at 
<a href="https://i.zwoastro.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/fc9b30ae6ea0aa4b331322439dee063c.pdf">ZWO ASI2600MC Air Manual</a></p>

<h3> Sky-Watcher HEQ5 Mount</h3>
<p> This is the equatorial mount and integrated tripod which holds and balances the telescope. It has two motorised axes which are entirely controlled by the
camera, and a polar scope for aligning it to the celestial sphere. The manual is available at 
<a href="https://inter-static.skywatcher.com/upfiles/en_download_caty01461887945.pdf">Sky-Watcher HEQ5 Mount Manual</a></p>

<h3> Askar 0.75x Reducer for 91F</h3>
<p>  This device changes the focal length of the telescope to
455mm and reduces the focal ratio to F5. This essentially provides a larger field of view and more light focussed per camera pixel (so less exposure time is needed). This comes at the
cost of less magnification and fine detail. </p>

<h3> Svbony SV12 Dew Heater</h3>
<p> This slips around the dew shield of the telescope, and is powered through the camera. To turn on, press the button on the cable. It slightly warms up
the objective lens to above the dew point, preventing droplets forming and scattering light. </p>

<h2> Using Northumberland or Thorrowgood for Astrophotography </h2>
<p>Both the Northumberland and Thorrowgood Telescopes can be used for astrophotography. 
We possess a 2"-to-T adapter to enable astrophotography using digital SLR
cameras, for which you will require appropriate adapters: a T-mount ring for your brand of camera. We
have a T-mount ring for Canon and Nikon cameras, but you will need to buy your own if you have a different brand.
This will allow "prime focus" astrophotography on Northumberland. Please be careful with the mounting, as heavy items
may fall out of the focuser if not secured properly. The weight of the camera could also cause the focus to slip,
so the focus lock may be useful. The focuser for Thorrowgood is of a non-standard size, thus the
only adapter we have is the push-fit eyepiece holder. It is not recommended to connect heavier cameras to this 1.25" adapter.</p>

